"""Move detailed audit prose to the supplement while keeping the scientific chain."""
from common import ROOT
import re
R=ROOT/'revision/review_v4';s=(R/'manuscript.md').read_text(encoding='utf-8');arch=[]
def change(start,end,new):
 global s
 a=s.index(start+'\n\n')+len(start)+2;b=s.index('\n\n'+end,a);arch.append(start+'\n\n'+s[a:b]);s=s[:a]+new.strip()+s[b:]
change('### 4.1 Calibration establishes a competitive weekly baseline','### 4.2 An exploratory northern effect is concentrated in summer','''Monthly EMOS has Week-1 CRPSS of 0.49–0.55 across the primary domains, falling by Week 4 to 0.038 in N, 0.002 in NE, 0.022 in SE and −0.007 in SW (Fig. 3). Direct 28-day rainfall-only EMOS improves on raw ECMWF over the full cross-validation period in every domain (Table 2), although adding spread and monthly windows worsens the simpler seasonal EMOS score in NE by 0.24 mm. Calibration flexibility is therefore not uniformly beneficial.

The homogeneous five-node benchmark changes domain-averaged 28-day CRPS by −0.16% of climatological CRPS (95% interval −0.35 to 0.03%) relative to BU-rain, and weekly CRPS by +0.11% (0.02–0.20%). None of its primary comparisons passes Holm adjustment. This defines the limited gain available before adding climate predictors.''')
change('### 4.2 An exploratory northern effect is concentrated in summer','### 4.3 Adding a fortnightly layer contributes little','''With seven nodes held fixed, H1 minus H0 changes 28-day CRPS by −0.31% of climatological CRPS (95% interval −0.70 to 0.06%; Holm-adjusted p = 0.268; Table 3). Absolute changes are −0.372 mm in N, +0.126 mm in NE, −0.056 mm in SE and −0.012 mm in SW (Fig. 4). The northern interval is −0.749 to −0.019 mm; the others include zero. N was highlighted after these results and remains exploratory.

The northern effect is concentrated in December to February, when H1 lowers CRPS from 47.69 to 46.14 mm. In March to May it instead increases CRPS from 16.93 to 17.11 mm (Supplementary Fig. S4). Monthly fitting already represents the seasonal cycle, so this contrast concerns additional climate information within seasonal windows. The direct H1 total also improves its northern point estimate but worsens the other domains; reconciliation moderates those losses.

H1 minus BU-rain changes domain-averaged CRPS by −0.51% (−1.00 to −0.05%), but its Holm-adjusted p value is 0.168. The northern difference is −0.541 mm (−0.951 to −0.154 mm); differences elsewhere include zero. None of the original primary tests therefore establishes a general advantage.''')
change('### 4.3 Adding a fortnightly layer contributes little','### 4.4 Distributional changes link rainfall regime to forecast scores','''Adding two fortnightly nodes within H1 changes normalised 28-day CRPS by +0.003% (−0.159 to 0.160%). Every domain's absolute change is below 0.02 mm. Weekly H1 minus BU-rain changes are −0.013, +0.021, +0.010 and +0.018 mm in N, NE, SE and SW, respectively. These small, mixed changes accompany the larger northern total gain. Fortnightly scores in Supplementary Table S3 likewise do not establish an automatic benefit from additional nodes.''')
change('### 4.4 Distributional changes link rainfall regime to forecast scores','### 4.5 Covariance and upper-tail diagnostics','''The largest distributional changes occur in N (Fig. 6; Table 4). Summing calibrated weeks widens the central 80% interval from the direct total's 90.76 mm to 109.68 mm, and raises coverage from 83.5 to 88.8%. H1 reduces the width to 101.29 mm and coverage to 84.1%. Its aggregate gain thus accompanies reduced overcoverage.

Relative to BU-rain, H1 lowers the reliability component of northern CRPS from 0.230 to 0.145 mm and potential CRPS from 20.857 to 20.401 mm. Both contribute to the total difference. Against H0, the potential-score reduction is 0.343 mm and the reliability reduction only 0.030 mm. The result cannot be described solely as a spread correction.

November to April contributes about 90% of northern direct 28-day CRPS despite containing roughly half the origins. Exceedances of the training seasonal 90th percentile account for 68 of 1060 origins (6.4%) and 20.7% of that score. These calendar-window thresholds are not separately calibrated conditional quantiles. Large wet-season amounts consequently dominate millimetre scores. Reliability and potential-score changes also differ elsewhere, as do the supplementary probability and joint-trajectory scores (Supplementary Table S4; Figs S1 and S2).''')
change('### 4.5 Covariance and upper-tail diagnostics','### 4.6 The northern gain is concentrated in a few years','''All 16 primary domain-season cases lie below the agreement line for covariance-predicted versus observed mean-square-error gain (Fig. 7). Observed minus predicted gain averages −4.96 percentage points for linear reconciliation and −5.21 points for nonnegative sample means; descriptive slopes are 0.12 and 0.07. Spearman correlations are 0.41 (95% interval −0.03 to 0.61) and 0.34 (−0.06 to 0.47). These diagnostics offer little support for selecting reconciliation from estimated covariance alone.

Upper-tail verification yields a different ranking from overall CRPS. H1 Brier skill scores are 0.218, 0.170, 0.225 and 0.101 in N, NE, SE and SW, compared with 0.187, 0.179, 0.218 and 0.107 for BU-rain. Thus each forecast has positive mean event skill, while aggregate information changes it in different directions (Table 4; Supplementary Table S7).''')
change('### 4.6 The northern gain is concentrated in a few years','### 4.7 Climate information also benefits weekly aggregation','''The northern H1 minus BU-rain contrast remains favourable under positive-part and signed-predictor quality-control refits, and under changes in sample size (Supplementary Tables S1 and S5). H1 minus H0 is also favourable under matched 11-sample ECC and historical ranks, although their absolute calibration differs (Supplementary Tables S2 and S9).

H1 improves on BU-rain in fourteen northern years (Fig. 9). The five years identified from the annual plot account for 87.0% of the net difference. Omitting their scores reduces the contrast to −0.094 mm and changes H1 minus H0 to +0.039 mm. Across every possible five-year omission, H1 minus BU-rain remains negative, but its size varies substantially (Supplementary Table S14). These omissions do not refit the models.

The northern H1-minus-BU-rain BCa interval is −0.966 to −0.174 mm, whereas the H1-minus-H0 interval includes zero with two- and three-year blocks. The domain-averaged contrast against BU-rain also includes zero with three-year blocks (Supplementary Table S10). The power-map diagnostic lowers fitting-window loss but increases inner-application loss because fitting and application populations differ; its details are confined to Supplementary Note 2, Tables S6 and S11, and Fig. S3.''')
change('### 3.5 Controls and transfer checks','### 3.6 Coherent mixtures and forecast selection','''Controls compare weekly climate information, an aggregate intercept, individual indices, monthly lags and dependence templates. Twenty additional full year-block permutations refit every nested fold using the shuffled joint climate series. Their Monte Carlo test assumes approximately exchangeable years. Supplementary Note 1 gives the fitting rules.

Forward fitting ends in 2014 and verifies 2015–2019; matching cross-validation scores to these same years separates period and design effects. BCa intervals, multiyear blocks and score omissions assess concentration in particular years. Geographical and ACCESS-S2 extensions retain the model settings. Their plans were recorded before their scores were calculated, but they address hypotheses motivated by existing results. Supplementary Tables S14–S18 retain all extension diagnostics.''')
# Keep a concise physical discussion; detailed audit numbers are already in Results.
start='The annual concentration reinforces this caution.'
a=s.index(start);b=s.index('Covariance diagnostics explain',a)
s=s[:a]+'''Annual concentration and forward validation constrain the interpretation. Removing five influential years nearly removes the northern gain against BU-rain and reverses the contrast against H0. Raw northern ECMWF leads the individual calibrated forecasts during 2015–2019 under both validation designs. Forward fitting further worsens H1 relative to its same-period cross-validation score. These findings point to sensitivity to the available training climate and evaluation period, without isolating a single cause.

'''+s[b:]
start='Covariance diagnostics explain';a=s.index(start);b=s.index('Dependence remains part',a)
s=s[:a]+'''Minimum-trace theory concerns linear unbiased forecasts under a specified covariance. Here covariance is estimated from finite training data, samples are projected onto a nonnegative set and forecasts are verified out of sample. The optimistic variance diagnostic therefore describes this implementation and dataset, not all covariance-based reconciliation. Results for particular aggregated autoregressive models (Neubauer and Filzmoser 2024) likewise do not determine the behaviour of these rainfall distributions.

'''+s[b:]
